/*
 * KCNA mock tests. Each test has 60 questions split across the domains by exam weight
 * (26 / 17 / 10 / 7), like the real exam.
 *
 * Question tuple: [domainKey, question, [options], explanation]
 * The FIRST option is the correct answer. The app shuffles the options (and the question
 * order) for every attempt, so there is no need to vary the position here.
 * Questions and options may use `code`; avoid * and | in the text.
 *
 * Style: most questions describe a situation and ask for the best answer, and the wrong
 * options are things a half-prepared candidate would plausibly choose. Keep that level when
 * adding questions.
 *
 * These questions are written for this project. They are not real exam questions.
 */
(DATA.mocks = DATA.mocks || {}).kcna = [
  {
    key: "test-1",
    title: "Mock test 1",
    minutes: 90,
    questions: [
      // ---- Kubernetes Fundamentals (26)
      ["fund", "The `kube-apiserver` of a cluster goes down while etcd and the nodes stay healthy. What happens to the applications that are already running?",
        ["Their pods keep running, but no changes can be made", "All pods are stopped until the API server returns", "The kubelets take over scheduling until the API server returns", "kubectl falls back to reading and writing etcd directly"],
        "Kubelets keep running the pods they already know about. Every change, including scheduling and scaling, goes through the API server, and nothing else talks to etcd."],
      ["fund", "A newly created pod stays `Pending`. It has no node assigned and there are no scheduling events at all. Which component is most likely not running?",
        ["`kube-scheduler`", "`kubelet`", "`kube-proxy`", "The container runtime"],
        "Assigning a node is the scheduler's job. A kubelet or runtime problem shows up only after a node has been chosen."],
      ["fund", "Which component is responsible for reporting a node's status and heartbeats to the API server?",
        ["`kubelet`", "`kube-proxy`", "The node controller in `kube-controller-manager`", "`cloud-controller-manager`"],
        "The kubelet reports. The node controller only reacts when the reports stop, by marking the node NotReady."],
      ["fund", "You delete one pod that belongs to a Deployment with 3 replicas. A replacement appears within seconds. What created the replacement pod object?",
        ["The ReplicaSet controller", "`kube-scheduler`", "The kubelet on the node where the old pod ran", "The HorizontalPodAutoscaler"],
        "The ReplicaSet controller sees 2 pods where 3 are wanted and creates one. The scheduler then places it; it does not create it."],
      ["fund", "A web server and a helper process must talk to each other over `localhost` and read the same files. How should they be deployed?",
        ["As two containers in one pod, with a shared volume", "As two pods placed on the same node with pod affinity", "As two Deployments connected by a ClusterIP Service", "As one DaemonSet with two pod templates"],
        "Containers in a pod share a network namespace, so localhost works, and can mount the same volume. Pods on the same node still have separate IPs."],
      ["fund", "An application connects to another application's pod by its IP address. After a rollout the connection fails. Why?",
        ["A recreated pod gets a new IP address, so clients should use a Service", "Pod IPs can be reached only from the same node", "Each container in the pod has its own IP, and the wrong one was used", "A Deployment's pods share the IP address of their node"],
        "Pod IPs are not stable. A Service provides a fixed virtual IP and DNS name in front of changing pods."],
      ["fund", "A clustered message broker needs every replica to keep the same name across restarts and to have its own disk. Which resource fits?",
        ["StatefulSet", "A Deployment whose pods all mount one PersistentVolumeClaim", "DaemonSet", "A Job with `completions` set to the number of brokers"],
        "A StatefulSet gives stable names (broker-0, broker-1) and one claim per replica through volumeClaimTemplates. A Deployment's pods would share one claim and have random names."],
      ["fund", "A log collector must run on every node, including nodes that are added to the cluster later. What is the simplest way to deploy it?",
        ["A DaemonSet", "A Deployment with replicas equal to the number of nodes", "A static pod manifest copied to each node", "A StatefulSet with pod anti-affinity"],
        "A DaemonSet adds a pod automatically for every new node. The other options need manual work whenever the node count changes."],
      ["fund", "A one-off data import must run to completion and be retried if it fails. It is not tied to any application pod. Which resource fits?",
        ["Job", "CronJob", "A Deployment with one replica", "An init container"],
        "A Job runs pods until they succeed and then stops. A Deployment would restart the import for ever, and a CronJob repeats it on a schedule."],
      ["fund", "An application must not start until its database accepts connections. The application image cannot be changed. What do you add to the pod?",
        ["An init container that waits for the database", "A readiness probe on the application container", "A sidecar container that restarts the application", "A liveness probe that checks the database"],
        "Init containers finish before the app containers start. A readiness probe only keeps traffic away; the application would still start and fail."],
      ["fund", "`kubectl get pods` shows a pod as `Running` with `0/1` in the READY column. What does this mean?",
        ["Its readiness probe is not passing yet", "The image is still being pulled", "The pod has not been assigned to a node", "The container was killed for using too much memory"],
        "Running means the container process exists. READY counts the containers that pass their readiness check and so receive Service traffic."],
      ["fund", "A team wants to record the Git commit and the on-call contact on each Deployment. The values are never used to select objects. Where do they belong?",
        ["Annotations", "Labels", "A ConfigMap in the same namespace", "The namespace name"],
        "Annotations hold descriptive data for people and tools. Labels are for identifying and selecting objects and have stricter length and character limits."],
      ["fund", "An administrator creates a Role in the namespace `dev`. Access to which of these resources can that Role NOT grant?",
        ["Nodes", "Pods", "Secrets", "ConfigMaps"],
        "A Role covers namespaced resources in its own namespace. Nodes are cluster-scoped and need a ClusterRole."],
      ["fund", "A pod will not start. Which single command shows its recent events together with the state and last exit reason of each container?",
        ["`kubectl describe pod <pod>`", "`kubectl get pod <pod> -o wide`", "`kubectl logs <pod>`", "`kubectl explain pod`"],
        "`describe` combines the object's status with its events. `logs` shows only what the application printed, and nothing if it never started."],
      ["fund", "What is a benefit of managing a cluster declaratively with manifests, compared with imperative commands?",
        ["Manifests can be versioned and re-applied to reach the same state", "Changes take effect faster because the API server is bypassed", "Objects created this way cannot be changed by other users", "No controllers are needed, because the manifest lists every step"],
        "Declarative files describe the end state, so they can be reviewed, versioned and re-applied. They still go through the API server and rely on controllers."],
      ["fund", "A process inside a container runs `ps` and sees only its own processes, although it uses the node's kernel. Which kernel feature makes this possible?",
        ["Namespaces", "cgroups", "seccomp", "Capabilities"],
        "The PID namespace limits what the process can see. Cgroups limit how much it can use; seccomp and capabilities limit what it may do."],
      ["fund", "One container on a node tries to use all of the node's CPU. Which kernel feature enforces the limit set in its pod spec?",
        ["cgroups", "Namespaces", "SELinux", "The overlay filesystem"],
        "Resource requests and limits are implemented with control groups."],
      ["fund", "Which sequence correctly describes how a container is started on a node?",
        ["The kubelet calls containerd through the CRI, and containerd calls runc", "The kubelet calls runc directly through the CRI, and runc pulls and unpacks the image", "The API server calls containerd, which informs the kubelet", "containerd calls the kubelet through the CRI to start runc"],
        "The kubelet speaks CRI to a high-level runtime (containerd or CRI-O), which pulls the image and uses a low-level OCI runtime such as runc."],
      ["fund", "An image is built with Podman, pushed to a Harbor registry and run by CRI-O on the nodes. Why do these different tools work together?",
        ["They all follow the OCI specifications", "They all use Docker Engine internally", "The CRI defines a common image format", "Kubernetes converts the image when the pod is scheduled"],
        "The OCI specifications standardise the image format, how registries exchange images and how runtimes run them. The CRI is only the kubelet-to-runtime API."],
      ["fund", "A storage vendor wants its product to be usable for Kubernetes volumes without adding code to Kubernetes itself. What should it provide?",
        ["A CSI driver", "A CRI plugin", "A CNI plugin", "A validating admission webhook"],
        "The Container Storage Interface lets storage systems be added as separately deployed drivers."],
      ["fund", "A monitoring tool's ServiceAccount must be able to read pods in every namespace. Which objects grant this?",
        ["A ClusterRole and a ClusterRoleBinding", "A ClusterRole and a RoleBinding in the tool's namespace", "A Role and a RoleBinding in the `default` namespace", "A Role and a ClusterRoleBinding"],
        "A ClusterRoleBinding grants a ClusterRole across all namespaces. A RoleBinding to a ClusterRole grants it in one namespace only, and a ClusterRoleBinding cannot refer to a Role."],
      ["fund", "Someone obtains a copy of an etcd backup from a cluster with default settings. What can they do with the Secrets in it?",
        ["Read them, because Secrets are only base64-encoded unless encryption at rest is configured", "Nothing, because Secrets are stored as one-way hashes", "Nothing, because Secrets are encrypted with the ServiceAccount signing key", "Nothing, because Secrets are kept in kubelet memory and not in etcd"],
        "Base64 is an encoding. Protecting Secrets needs encryption at rest, restricted access to etcd and its backups, and tight RBAC."],
      ["fund", "A namespace has a ResourceQuota on memory. Pods that declare no memory request are rejected. How do you fix this without editing every pod?",
        ["Add a LimitRange that sets default requests and limits", "Raise the ResourceQuota until the pods are accepted again", "Give the pods a higher PriorityClass", "Add a PodDisruptionBudget"],
        "With a quota on a resource, every pod must declare it. A LimitRange fills in defaults for containers that declare nothing."],
      ["fund", "A container has a CPU request of `500m` and a CPU limit of `1`, and a memory request of `256Mi` with a memory limit of `512Mi`. Which QoS class does its pod get?",
        ["Burstable", "Guaranteed", "BestEffort", "Limited"],
        "Guaranteed needs requests equal to limits for every container. Some requests or limits set, but not equal, gives Burstable."],
      ["fund", "A container tries to use more CPU than its CPU limit allows. What happens?",
        ["It is throttled and keeps running", "It is killed and restarted", "Its pod is evicted from the node", "The limit is raised if the node has spare CPU"],
        "CPU is compressible, so the container is only slowed down. Memory is not: going over a memory limit gets the container OOMKilled."],
      ["fund", "GPU nodes must run only GPU workloads, and GPU workloads must run only on GPU nodes. What do you configure?",
        ["A taint on the GPU nodes, plus a toleration and node affinity on the GPU pods", "Only a `nodeSelector` on the GPU pods", "Only a taint on the GPU nodes and a toleration on the GPU pods", "A PodDisruptionBudget for the GPU pods"],
        "The taint keeps other pods away. The toleration only permits GPU pods to land there; node affinity (or a nodeSelector) is what makes them go there and nowhere else."],

      // ---- Container Orchestration (17)
      ["orch", "Front-end pods must reach a back-end API that should not be reachable from outside the cluster. Which Service type fits the API?",
        ["ClusterIP", "NodePort", "LoadBalancer", "ExternalName"],
        "ClusterIP is internal only. NodePort and LoadBalancer both open the Service to traffic from outside."],
      ["orch", "Clients of a database StatefulSet must be able to address each replica individually by a DNS name. What do you create?",
        ["A headless Service (`clusterIP: None`)", "A ClusterIP Service with session affinity", "A NodePort Service for each replica", "An ExternalName Service"],
        "A headless Service gives each StatefulSet pod its own DNS record, such as `db-0.db`. A normal Service hides the pods behind one virtual IP."],
      ["orch", "A pod in the namespace `web` calls `http://api` and the name does not resolve. The Service `api` exists in the namespace `backend`. What is the fix?",
        ["Call `api.backend`, because a short name resolves only within the pod's own namespace", "Restart CoreDNS, because Services in other namespaces are cached", "Create an Ingress, because Services cannot be reached across namespaces", "Change the Service type to NodePort"],
        "The short name is tried in the caller's own namespace. Across namespaces use `<service>.<namespace>` or the full name."],
      ["orch", "A namespace has one NetworkPolicy. It selects the pods labelled `app=db` and allows ingress from pods labelled `app=api`. What applies to the other pods in the namespace?",
        ["Nothing changes for them: they still accept all traffic", "They now accept traffic only from `app=api` pods", "All traffic to them is denied", "Their outgoing traffic is blocked"],
        "A policy isolates only the pods it selects. Pods that no policy selects stay open."],
      ["orch", "You create a NetworkPolicy and nothing changes: traffic that should be blocked still gets through. What is the most likely reason?",
        ["The cluster's CNI plugin does not enforce NetworkPolicy", "The pods must be restarted before a policy applies", "NetworkPolicies apply to Services, not to pods", "`kube-proxy` is running in iptables mode"],
        "The API server stores the object, but enforcement is the CNI plugin's job. Some plugins, such as plain Flannel, do not implement it."],
      ["orch", "An Ingress has been created, but `kubectl get ingress` shows no ADDRESS and requests to the host fail. The backend Service and its pods work. What is the most likely cause?",
        ["No ingress controller handles this Ingress", "The backend Service must be of type LoadBalancer", "`kube-proxy` does not support Ingress in this mode", "The Ingress is in a different namespace from CoreDNS"],
        "An Ingress is only a set of rules. Without a controller that claims it, nothing implements them."],
      ["orch", "A platform team owns the load balancer and its TLS certificates. Application teams should manage only their own routing rules, in their own namespaces. Which API is designed for this split?",
        ["The Gateway API, with a shared Gateway and an HTTPRoute for each team", "One Ingress per team, configured with controller annotations", "A NodePort Service per team", "An ExternalName Service per team"],
        "The Gateway API is role-oriented: the Gateway belongs to the operator and routes attach to it from application namespaces."],
      ["orch", "A company wants encryption between all services, automatic retries and per-request metrics, without changing application code. What should it adopt?",
        ["A service mesh", "NetworkPolicies", "An ingress controller", "A CNI plugin with a larger MTU"],
        "A mesh adds these features in proxies beside the applications. NetworkPolicy only allows or blocks traffic, and an ingress controller handles traffic entering the cluster."],
      ["orch", "In a sidecar-based service mesh, what actually intercepts and forwards the requests between services?",
        ["The proxy container in each pod", "The control plane of the service mesh", "`kube-proxy`", "CoreDNS"],
        "The proxies are the data plane. The control plane only configures them and issues certificates."],
      ["orch", "A code review finds a SQL injection flaw in an application. Which of the 4Cs of cloud native security does this belong to?",
        ["Code", "Container", "Cluster", "Cloud"],
        "The innermost layer covers the application's own code and its dependencies. The outer layers cannot fully protect against a flaw there."],
      ["orch", "A namespace must reject pods that run privileged or use the host network, while still accepting ordinary workloads that have not been hardened. Which Pod Security Admission label fits?",
        ["`pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: baseline`", "`pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: privileged`", "`pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted`", "`pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: baseline`"],
        "Baseline blocks known privilege escalations and `enforce` rejects violations. `warn` and `audit` only report, and Privileged allows everything."],
      ["orch", "An attacker who has compromised a container tries to download and store a tool inside it. Which `securityContext` setting blocks writes to the container's image filesystem?",
        ["`readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`", "`runAsNonRoot: true`", "`allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`", "`seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault`"],
        "A read-only root filesystem stops changes to the image's files. The other settings restrict the user, privilege gain and system calls."],
      ["orch", "A PersistentVolumeClaim stays `Pending`. Its events say that no persistent volumes are available for the claim and no storage class is set. What is the cause?",
        ["No PersistentVolume matches and there is no default StorageClass", "No pod uses the claim yet", "The nodes have no free disk space", "The claim asks for the `ReadWriteOnce` access mode, which needs a StorageClass"],
        "A claim binds to a matching existing volume or gets one from a StorageClass. With neither, it waits."],
      ["orch", "With dynamic provisioning, what creates the PersistentVolume for a new claim?",
        ["The provisioner named in the StorageClass", "The cluster administrator, by hand", "The kubelet on the node where the pod runs", "`kube-scheduler`"],
        "The StorageClass names a provisioner, which creates the storage and the PersistentVolume object when a claim asks for it."],
      ["orch", "Pods on several different nodes must write to the same volume at the same time. Which access mode does the volume need?",
        ["ReadWriteMany", "ReadWriteOnce", "ReadOnlyMany", "ReadWriteOncePod"],
        "ReadWriteOnce allows a single node. ReadWriteMany needs storage that supports it, such as a network file system."],
      ["orch", "A pod is in `CrashLoopBackOff`. `kubectl logs <pod>` prints almost nothing, because the container has only just been restarted. How do you see why the last attempt failed?",
        ["`kubectl logs <pod> --previous`", "`kubectl logs <pod> --follow`", "`kubectl get pod <pod> -o wide`", "`kubectl top pod <pod>`"],
        "`--previous` shows the output of the container instance that exited, not of the one that has just started."],
      ["orch", "A pod is `Pending` with the event `0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu`, yet `kubectl top nodes` shows the nodes are almost idle. Why?",
        ["Scheduling counts the CPU requests of placed pods, not actual usage", "The usage figures are out of date until metrics-server is restarted", "The nodes are tainted", "The kubelets have stopped reporting"],
        "The scheduler adds up requests. A node can be idle and still have all of its CPU reserved by the requests of its pods."],

      // ---- Cloud Native Application Delivery (10)
      ["deliv", "In a cluster managed with GitOps, an engineer changes the replica count of a Deployment by hand with kubectl. What happens next?",
        ["The GitOps agent detects the drift and restores the state defined in Git", "The change is committed back to the Git repository", "The agent stops syncing that application until the change is approved", "Nothing: manual changes take priority over Git"],
        "Git is the source of truth and the agent reconciles continuously, so a manual change is treated as drift."],
      ["deliv", "A security team does not want the CI system to hold credentials that can change the production cluster. Which deployment approach avoids this?",
        ["A pull-based approach, with an agent such as Argo CD or Flux in the cluster", "Running `kubectl apply` in the last pipeline stage", "Running `helm upgrade` from the pipeline", "Mounting the kubeconfig into the pipeline from a secret store"],
        "With pull-based delivery the agent inside the cluster fetches from Git. In the other three options the pipeline still needs access to the cluster."],
      ["deliv", "A third-party application is published as a versioned package with many configurable parameters. Which tool is built to install and upgrade it?",
        ["Helm", "Kustomize", "`kubectl apply`", "Argo Workflows"],
        "A Helm chart is a versioned package with values to configure it, and Helm tracks each installation as a release."],
      ["deliv", "A team keeps plain YAML manifests and wants dev and prod variants that differ in a few fields, without introducing a template language. What should it use?",
        ["Kustomize overlays", "A Helm chart", "A separate copy of the manifests for each environment", "A ConfigMap for each environment"],
        "Kustomize patches a common base for each environment and is built into kubectl. Helm would bring in templates; copies drift apart."],
      ["deliv", "A Deployment has 4 replicas, `maxSurge: 1` and `maxUnavailable: 0`. What is true during a rolling update?",
        ["There are at most 5 pods, and never fewer than 4 available", "There are at most 4 pods, and at least 3 available", "All 4 pods are replaced at the same moment", "There are 8 pods until the update is finished"],
        "`maxSurge` is how many extra pods may exist and `maxUnavailable` how many may be missing. Here each new pod must be ready before an old one is removed."],
      ["deliv", "A new version must be tried by about 5% of real users before everyone gets it. Which release strategy is this?",
        ["Canary", "Blue/green", "Recreate", "Rolling update"],
        "A canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version. Blue/green switches all traffic at once."],
      ["deliv", "Two versions of an application must never run at the same time, and a short downtime is acceptable. Which strategy fits?",
        ["Recreate", "RollingUpdate", "Canary", "Blue/green"],
        "Recreate stops every old pod before starting new ones. The other three all run both versions side by side for a while."],
      ["deliv", "A rollout is stuck: the new pods are in `ImagePullBackOff` because of a typing mistake in the image tag. What is the quickest way back to the working version?",
        ["`kubectl rollout undo deployment/<name>`", "Delete the Deployment and create it again", "Scale the Deployment to zero and back", "Delete the failing pods"],
        "`rollout undo` returns to the previous ReplicaSet. Deleting pods or scaling only recreates pods with the same broken image."],
      ["deliv", "An application needs 90 seconds to start. Its liveness probe keeps killing it before it is up. What is the right fix?",
        ["Add a startup probe", "Add a readiness probe", "Increase the number of replicas", "Remove the resource limits"],
        "A startup probe holds back the liveness probe until the application has started. A readiness probe does not stop liveness restarts."],
      ["deliv", "An application cannot serve requests for a few seconds while it reloads its cache. It must not be restarted during that time. Which probe handles this?",
        ["A readiness probe", "A liveness probe", "A startup probe", "A `preStop` hook"],
        "A failing readiness probe takes the pod out of the Service endpoints without restarting it. A failing liveness probe would restart the container."],

      // ---- Cloud Native Architecture (7)
      ["arch", "Users report that checkout is slow. A request passes through 12 services. Which kind of telemetry shows where the time is spent for a single request?",
        ["Distributed traces", "Metrics", "Logs", "Kubernetes events"],
        "A trace records the path of one request as timed spans across services. Metrics show that latency is high, not where."],
      ["arch", "A batch job runs for 20 seconds and exits. Prometheus scrapes every 60 seconds and usually misses it. What is the intended solution?",
        ["The job pushes its metrics to a Pushgateway", "Lower the scrape interval to one second", "Send the metrics to Alertmanager", "Install node exporter in the job's container"],
        "Prometheus pulls. The Pushgateway exists for short-lived jobs that cannot wait to be scraped."],
      ["arch", "A cautious organisation will adopt only CNCF projects that are proven in production, have mature governance and have completed a security audit. Which maturity level should it look for?",
        ["Graduated", "Incubating", "Sandbox", "Archived"],
        "Graduated is the highest level. Incubating projects are used in production but have not met all graduation criteria."],
      ["arch", "Which body decides whether a project moves from Incubating to Graduated in the CNCF?",
        ["The Technical Oversight Committee", "The Governing Board", "A Technical Advisory Group", "The End User Technical Advisory Board"],
        "The TOC decides. TAGs review and advise, and the Governing Board deals with business matters."],
      ["arch", "A security team wants an alert when a shell is started inside a running production container. Which CNCF project is designed for this?",
        ["Falco", "Trivy", "Kyverno", "Open Policy Agent"],
        "Falco watches system calls at runtime. Trivy scans images before they run, and Kyverno and OPA decide what may be created."],
      ["arch", "An engineer wants to add a significant new feature to Kubernetes itself. What is the expected first step?",
        ["Write a Kubernetes Enhancement Proposal (KEP) with the owning SIG", "Open a pull request with the code against `kubernetes/kubernetes`", "Ask the CNCF Technical Oversight Committee for approval", "Ask the CNCF Governing Board to vote on it"],
        "Substantial changes start as a KEP owned by a SIG. The TOC and the Governing Board do not decide Kubernetes features."],
      ["arch", "A team wants to instrument its services once and be able to change tracing backends later without touching the code. What should it instrument with?",
        ["OpenTelemetry", "The Jaeger client libraries", "Prometheus client libraries", "Fluent Bit"],
        "OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral standard: its SDKs and the OTLP protocol can send data to any compatible backend."]
    ]
  },
  {
    key: "test-2",
    title: "Mock test 2",
    minutes: 90,
    questions: [
      // ---- Kubernetes Fundamentals (26)
      ["fund", "A control plane has three etcd members and one of them fails. What is the effect on the cluster?",
        ["It keeps working, because two of three members still form a majority", "It becomes read-only until the member returns", "It stops accepting requests until the member is replaced", "It keeps working only if a fourth member is added"],
        "etcd needs a majority (quorum). Three members tolerate the loss of one; a second failure would stop writes."],
      ["fund", "All control plane nodes of a self-managed cluster are lost. Which backup is needed to bring back the cluster's objects?",
        ["A snapshot of etcd", "The kubelet directories of the worker nodes", "The container images of the control plane components", "The scheduler's configuration file"],
        "All cluster state lives in etcd. Images and configuration can be reinstalled, but the objects cannot be rebuilt without the data."],
      ["fund", "A Service's ClusterIP cannot be reached from pods on one particular node, while it works from all other nodes. Which component on that node do you check first?",
        ["`kube-proxy`", "CoreDNS", "`kube-scheduler`", "etcd"],
        "kube-proxy programs the rules that send ClusterIP traffic to pods on each node. A problem on a single node points to that node's kube-proxy."],
      ["fund", "On a bare-metal cluster a Service of type LoadBalancer stays at `<pending>` for its external IP. Why?",
        ["Nothing in the cluster provides load balancers", "`kube-proxy` is not running", "The Service has no selector", "CoreDNS has not yet created a record for the Service"],
        "The Service only asks for a load balancer. A cloud controller, or an add-on such as MetalLB, has to supply one."],
      ["fund", "You delete the `kube-scheduler` pod with kubectl and it reappears at once. Its manifest is in `/etc/kubernetes/manifests` on the node. Why does it come back?",
        ["It is a static pod, run by the kubelet from that directory", "A Deployment in `kube-system` recreates it", "A DaemonSet recreates it", "The scheduler registers itself again through the API server"],
        "Static pods are owned by the kubelet. The object you see in the API is only a mirror; to remove the pod, remove the file."],
      ["fund", "Which statement best describes how Kubernetes controllers work?",
        ["They continuously reconcile actual state with desired state", "They run once when an object is created and then exit", "They carry out the steps listed in a manifest, in the given order", "They act only when a user runs a kubectl command"],
        "This reconciliation loop is why the system heals itself without anyone asking."],
      ["fund", "A user is authenticated and RBAC allows them to create pods, but their pod is rejected because the namespace forbids privileged containers. Which stage of request handling rejected it?",
        ["Admission control", "Authentication", "Authorization", "Scheduling"],
        "Admission runs after authentication and authorization and can reject or change an object based on its content."],
      ["fund", "A team wants to manage `Database` objects with kubectl, as if they were built-in resources, and have something act on them. What does it need?",
        ["A CustomResourceDefinition and a controller", "A ConfigMap named `Database`", "A `Database` annotation on an ordinary Deployment", "A new namespace for databases"],
        "The CRD adds the resource type to the API. Without a controller the objects would be stored but nothing would happen."],
      ["fund", "What does an operator do that a Helm chart alone does not?",
        ["It keeps managing the application after it is installed", "It installs the application's manifests in the cluster", "It lets the application be configured with parameters", "It records the versions that have been installed"],
        "A chart installs and upgrades manifests on request. An operator is a running controller that reacts to what happens to the application."],
      ["fund", "After a cluster upgrade, applying an old manifest fails with `no matches for kind` for its beta API version. What has happened?",
        ["That API version was removed; update the manifest", "The CRD for the resource was deleted during the upgrade", "The user no longer has permission for the resource", "The manifest must now be applied with `--force`"],
        "Deprecated beta APIs are removed after a notice period. Check the release notes before upgrading and migrate manifests to the stable version."],
      ["fund", "You change the image of a Deployment. What does the Deployment controller do?",
        ["It creates a new ReplicaSet and scales it up while scaling the old one down", "It edits the image of the existing pods in place", "It deletes the Deployment and creates a new one", "It tells each kubelet to pull the image and restart the containers"],
        "Each version of the pod template has its own ReplicaSet. The old one is kept at zero replicas, which is what makes a rollback possible."],
      ["fund", "An application can only write its logs to a file inside the container. The logs must be shipped to a central system without changing the application. What do you add?",
        ["A sidecar container that reads a shared volume", "An init container that copies the file", "A DaemonSet that reads the container's stdout", "A liveness probe that reads the file"],
        "A sidecar runs for the life of the pod and can share a volume with the application. A node agent sees only stdout and stderr."],
      ["fund", "`kubectl get pods` returns `No resources found`, although the application is running in the namespace `shop`. What is missing from the command?",
        ["`-n shop`", "`--all`", "`-o wide`", "`--show-labels`"],
        "Without a namespace flag kubectl looks in the current context's namespace, usually `default`. `-A` would list all namespaces."],
      ["fund", "Which command lists the pods that have both the label `tier=frontend` and the label `env=prod`?",
        ["`kubectl get pods -l tier=frontend,env=prod`", "`kubectl get pods --field-selector tier=frontend,env=prod`", "`kubectl get pods -n frontend,prod`", "`kubectl describe pods tier=frontend env=prod`"],
        "A comma in a label selector means AND. Field selectors work on fields such as `status.phase`, not on labels."],
      ["fund", "You need a correct Deployment manifest as a starting point, without creating anything in the cluster. Which command gives you one?",
        ["`kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml`", "`kubectl get deployment web -o yaml`", "`kubectl explain deployment`", "`kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --validate=true --server-side --force-conflicts`"],
        "A client-side dry run prints the object kubectl would send. `get` needs an existing object, and `explain` prints documentation."],
      ["fund", "Why is the isolation between containers generally considered weaker than between virtual machines?",
        ["Containers share the host's kernel", "Containers have no filesystem isolation", "Containers always run as root on the host", "Containers cannot be given separate network stacks"],
        "A VM has its own kernel behind a hypervisor. Containers rely on kernel features of one shared kernel."],
      ["fund", "A Deployment uses the image `shop/web:latest`. After a node is replaced, pods on different nodes run different code. What is the cause, and what is the fix?",
        ["A tag can be moved; refer to the image by digest", "The registry is faulty; move to another registry", "The kubelet's image cache is damaged; restart the kubelets", "Image layers were shared wrongly; disable layer sharing"],
        "`latest` is only a tag, and nodes that pulled at different times got different content. A digest always names the same image."],
      ["fund", "Two images on a node are built from the same base image. How are their common layers handled?",
        ["They are stored once and shared by both images", "They are downloaded twice, once for each image", "They are merged into one combined image", "Only one of the two images can run at a time"],
        "Layers are identified by their content and are read-only, so they can be shared safely."],
      ["fund", "Right after a cluster is installed, the nodes are `NotReady` with the message that the network plugin is not ready. What is missing?",
        ["A CNI plugin", "A CSI driver", "A container runtime", "An ingress controller"],
        "Kubernetes does not ship pod networking. Until a CNI plugin is installed, nodes cannot become Ready and pods get no IP addresses."],
      ["fund", "A cluster's nodes are moved from containerd to CRI-O and the kubelet needs no code change. Which interface makes this possible?",
        ["CRI", "CNI", "CSI", "OCI distribution"],
        "The kubelet talks to any runtime that implements the Container Runtime Interface."],
      ["fund", "An application in a pod needs to list the pods in its own namespace through the Kubernetes API. What is the correct way to give it access?",
        ["Bind a Role with the needed verbs to the pod's ServiceAccount", "Mount an administrator's kubeconfig into the pod as a Secret volume", "Let the pod use the node's kubelet credentials", "Allow anonymous requests on the API server"],
        "Pods authenticate with their ServiceAccount token. A Role bound to that ServiceAccount grants exactly what is needed."],
      ["fund", "A user is bound to two Roles in one namespace. One allows `get` on pods; the other allows `delete` on pods. What can the user do?",
        ["Both get and delete pods, because permissions add up", "Only get pods, because the more restrictive Role wins", "Only what the Role that was bound first allows", "Neither, because the Roles conflict"],
        "RBAC is additive and has no deny rules. The effective permissions are the union of all bound roles."],
      ["fund", "An application reads a ConfigMap value through an environment variable. The ConfigMap is updated, but the application still uses the old value. Why?",
        ["Environment variables are set only when the container starts", "ConfigMap changes take up to an hour to reach pods", "ConfigMaps cannot be changed after they are created", "The Deployment must be deleted and created again to read a ConfigMap"],
        "Only values mounted as files are refreshed in a running pod. A `kubectl rollout restart` picks up new environment values."],
      ["fund", "Several teams share one cluster. What stops one team from using up all of the cluster's CPU?",
        ["A ResourceQuota on each team's namespace", "A LimitRange in each team's namespace", "A PodDisruptionBudget for each application", "A NetworkPolicy between the namespaces"],
        "A ResourceQuota caps the namespace total. A LimitRange bounds single containers, so a team could still create many of them."],
      ["fund", "A node needs a kernel update and a reboot. Which sequence keeps applications available?",
        ["Drain the node, update it, then uncordon it", "Cordon the node, update it, then uncordon it", "Delete the Node object, update the machine, then let it rejoin", "Taint the node with `NoSchedule`, update it, then remove the taint"],
        "`drain` evicts the pods so they start elsewhere first. Cordon and a NoSchedule taint only stop new pods; the running ones would go down with the reboot."],
      ["fund", "Which statement about component versions in a cluster is correct?",
        ["A kubelet must not be newer than the `kube-apiserver`, but it may be older", "A kubelet may be one minor version newer than the `kube-apiserver`", "All components must have exactly the same patch version", "The `kube-scheduler` may be two minor versions newer than the `kube-apiserver`"],
        "The API server is upgraded first and is always the newest component. Kubelets may lag behind by a few minor versions."],

      // ---- Container Orchestration (17)
      ["orch", "A test cluster on bare metal has no load balancer. A Service must be reachable from outside at `<node IP>:<port>`. Which Service type do you use?",
        ["NodePort", "ClusterIP", "ExternalName", "Headless"],
        "NodePort opens the same port (30000 to 32767 by default) on every node."],
      ["orch", "A Service has the selector `app=web`, but the pods are labelled `app=webapp`. What is the effect?",
        ["The Service has no endpoints, so requests to it fail", "Traffic goes to every pod in the namespace", "The API server rejects the Service", "Traffic goes to the pods whose names contain `web`"],
        "A Service is valid without matching pods; it just has nothing to send to. Checking the endpoints is the first step when a Service does not respond."],
      ["orch", "Which name reaches the Service `db` in the namespace `data` from a pod in any namespace?",
        ["`db.data.svc.cluster.local`", "`db`", "`data.db.svc.cluster.local`", "`db.svc.data.cluster.local`"],
        "The pattern is `<service>.<namespace>.svc.<cluster domain>`. The short name `db` works only from inside the `data` namespace."],
      ["orch", "Two pods run on different nodes. According to the Kubernetes network model, how do they communicate?",
        ["Directly, using each other's pod IP addresses, without NAT", "Only through a Service", "Through network address translation performed by each node", "Through the API server"],
        "Every pod has its own IP and all pods can reach each other. Services are a convenience on top, not a requirement."],
      ["orch", "During a rollout a new pod becomes ready. How does it start to receive traffic sent to the Service?",
        ["It is added to the Service's EndpointSlices", "CoreDNS adds a record for the pod to the Service name", "The scheduler registers the pod with the Service", "The ingress controller must be reloaded"],
        "The EndpointSlice controller tracks ready pods that match the selector, and kube-proxy follows it."],
      ["orch", "A database that speaks a plain TCP protocol must be reachable from outside the cluster. Why is an Ingress not suitable?",
        ["The Ingress API covers only HTTP and HTTPS routing", "An Ingress cannot refer to a Service that has a selector", "An Ingress works only inside the cluster", "An Ingress needs a NodePort in the range 80 to 443"],
        "For other protocols use a LoadBalancer or NodePort Service, or a Gateway API route for TCP."],
      ["orch", "A team wants to send 90% of requests to version 1 and 10% to version 2. Why is the Gateway API a better fit than Ingress?",
        ["An HTTPRoute has weighted backends as a standard field", "Ingress cannot route to more than one Service", "The Gateway API needs no controller", "Ingress has been removed from current Kubernetes versions"],
        "Ingress is frozen at basic host and path routing. The Gateway API has traffic splitting built in and portable between implementations."],
      ["orch", "Which NetworkPolicy denies all incoming traffic to every pod in a namespace?",
        ["One with `podSelector: {}`, `policyTypes: [Ingress]` and no ingress rules", "One with `podSelector: {}` and `ingress: [{}]`", "None is needed: incoming traffic is denied by default", "One with `podSelector: {}`, `policyTypes: [Egress]` and no egress rules"],
        "An empty podSelector selects every pod, and with no rules nothing is allowed. `ingress: [{}]` is the opposite: one rule that allows everything."],
      ["orch", "Database pods should accept connections only from the `api` pods in the same namespace. Which object achieves this?",
        ["A NetworkPolicy on the database pods that allows ingress from `app=api` pods", "A ClusterIP Service in front of the database", "A Role and RoleBinding that let only the `api` ServiceAccount get the database pods", "The Restricted Pod Security Standard on the namespace"],
        "NetworkPolicy controls which pods may connect. A ClusterIP Service is reachable from the whole cluster, and RBAC governs the Kubernetes API, not pod traffic."],
      ["orch", "What is a typical cost of a sidecar-based service mesh?",
        ["Every pod runs an extra proxy that uses CPU and memory", "Every application must be rewritten to use the mesh's library", "NetworkPolicies can no longer be used", "Each service needs a node of its own"],
        "The mesh's features come from the proxy beside each application, and that proxy is not free."],
      ["orch", "A security team wants to find out which existing workloads would violate the Restricted Pod Security Standard, without blocking anything yet. What should it do?",
        ["Label the namespaces with `audit` and `warn` set to `restricted`", "Label the namespaces with the `enforce` mode set to `restricted`", "Set the namespaces to the Privileged level", "Delete the pods and see which ones cannot be recreated"],
        "`audit` writes violations to the audit log and `warn` shows them to users, while pods are still admitted. `enforce` would reject them."],
      ["orch", "A company wants its clusters to run only images that its own build pipeline produced. Which approach enforces this?",
        ["Sign the images in the pipeline and verify signatures at admission", "Scan every image for vulnerabilities before it is pushed to the registry", "Keep the images in a private registry", "Apply the Restricted Pod Security Standard"],
        "A signature proves who produced an image, and admission-time verification blocks anything unsigned. Scanning finds known flaws but says nothing about origin."],
      ["orch", "A CI pipeline must fail when an image contains known critical vulnerabilities. Which tool does this job?",
        ["Trivy", "Falco", "Cosign", "kube-bench"],
        "Trivy scans images for known CVEs. Falco detects behaviour at runtime, Cosign signs, and kube-bench checks cluster configuration against the CIS benchmark."],
      ["orch", "A developer deleted a PersistentVolumeClaim by mistake and the data was destroyed. Which reclaim policy would have kept it?",
        ["Retain", "Delete", "Bound", "Immediate"],
        "With Retain the volume and its data stay until an administrator cleans up. Delete, the usual default for dynamic volumes, removes the storage."],
      ["orch", "Two containers in a pod exchange files. The files do not need to survive the pod. Which volume type is appropriate?",
        ["`emptyDir`", "A PersistentVolumeClaim", "`hostPath`", "`configMap`"],
        "An emptyDir lives as long as the pod and can be mounted by all its containers. hostPath ties the pod to a node and is a security risk."],
      ["orch", "A pod is in `ImagePullBackOff`. The image name and tag are correct, and the image is in a private registry. What is most likely missing?",
        ["An `imagePullSecrets` entry", "A memory request on the container", "A readiness probe", "A Service in front of the pod"],
        "Private registries need credentials, supplied through a pull Secret on the pod or its ServiceAccount."],
      ["orch", "A container keeps restarting. Its last state shows exit code 137 with the reason `OOMKilled`. What should you do?",
        ["Raise its memory limit or fix its memory use", "Raise its CPU limit so that it is throttled less", "Add more replicas", "Add a liveness probe"],
        "137 is 128 + 9: the process was killed for going over its memory limit. CPU limits only throttle."],

      // ---- Cloud Native Application Delivery (10)
      ["deliv", "A team keeps its manifests in Git, and an engineer runs `kubectl apply` from a laptop after every merge. Which GitOps principle is not met?",
        ["The state is pulled and reconciled automatically by an agent", "The desired state is declarative", "The desired state is versioned", "The desired state is kept in a way that preserves its history"],
        "Storing manifests in Git covers the declarative and versioned principles. GitOps also needs an agent that applies them and corrects drift without a person."],
      ["deliv", "Which pair of tools are both GitOps tools that keep a cluster in sync with a Git repository?",
        ["Argo CD and Flux", "Helm and Kustomize", "Jenkins and Tekton", "Prometheus and Grafana"],
        "Helm and Kustomize produce manifests; Jenkins and Tekton run pipelines. Argo CD and Flux reconcile clusters with Git."],
      ["deliv", "In a regulated company every change is built and tested automatically and is always ready to release, but a person must approve each production release. What is this practice called?",
        ["Continuous delivery", "Continuous deployment", "Continuous integration", "GitOps"],
        "Continuous deployment has no manual gate. Continuous integration covers only merging, building and testing."],
      ["deliv", "A company's cloud networks and databases were created by hand in a web console, and staging no longer matches production. Which practice addresses this?",
        ["Infrastructure as Code", "Stricter RBAC", "A service mesh", "Cluster autoscaling"],
        "Describing infrastructure in versioned files makes environments repeatable and differences visible in review."],
      ["deliv", "A `helm upgrade` has broken an application. How do you return the release to its previous revision?",
        ["`helm rollback <release> <revision>`", "`helm uninstall <release> --keep-history`", "`helm repo update`", "`helm template <release>`"],
        "Helm records each upgrade as a revision of the release and can return to an earlier one."],
      ["deliv", "Where does a current version of Helm keep the record of a release?",
        ["In Secrets in the release's namespace", "In the Tiller pod in `kube-system`", "Only in a file on the user's machine", "In an annotation on each object it installed"],
        "Tiller was the server-side part of Helm 2 and was removed in Helm 3. The cluster itself holds the release history."],
      ["deliv", "What is the main drawback of a blue/green deployment compared with a rolling update?",
        ["It needs capacity for two complete environments", "It always causes downtime during the switch", "It cannot be rolled back", "It works only for stateless applications"],
        "Both versions run in full until the switch. In return, switching and rolling back are instant."],
      ["deliv", "During a rolling update the new pods never become ready. With the default settings, what happens?",
        ["The rollout stalls, and most of the old pods keep serving traffic", "The old pods are removed anyway, causing an outage", "The Deployment rolls back to the previous version automatically", "The Deployment is scaled to zero"],
        "Old pods are removed only as new ones become ready. Kubernetes does not roll back by itself; you run `kubectl rollout undo`."],
      ["deliv", "A healthy but slow application is restarted every few minutes under load. `kubectl describe pod` shows `Liveness probe failed` events. What is the likely cause?",
        ["The liveness probe is too strict for the application under load", "The readiness probe is missing", "The container is running out of memory and is being OOMKilled", "The image tag is wrong"],
        "A liveness probe that fails only because the application answers slowly causes needless restarts, which make the load problem worse."],
      ["deliv", "`kubectl logs web-7d4b` returns an error asking you to choose a container. What do you add?",
        ["`-c <container>`", "`--previous`", "`-n <container>`", "`--follow`"],
        "A pod with several containers needs `-c` to pick one, or `--all-containers` for all of them."],

      // ---- Cloud Native Architecture (7)
      ["arch", "Which Prometheus metric type suits the number of requests currently being processed, a value that goes up and down?",
        ["Gauge", "Counter", "Histogram", "Summary"],
        "A counter only increases. A gauge holds a current value that can rise and fall."],
      ["arch", "One outage triggers 50 separate alerts. The team wants a single grouped notification in its chat channel. Which component provides this?",
        ["Alertmanager", "Prometheus recording rules", "Pushgateway", "kube-state-metrics"],
        "Prometheus evaluates alert rules; Alertmanager groups, silences and routes the resulting alerts."],
      ["arch", "A service has an availability SLO of 99.9% and has used up its error budget for the month. What does SRE practice suggest?",
        ["Put reliability work ahead of new features until the budget recovers", "Raise the SLO to 99.99% so that the error budget becomes larger", "Stop measuring until the next month", "Nothing: error budgets apply only to SLAs"],
        "The error budget is the unreliability the SLO allows. Spending it all is the signal to slow down change and stabilise."],
      ["arch", "A contract promises customers 99.5% availability and gives service credits if this is missed. What is this?",
        ["A Service Level Agreement (SLA)", "A Service Level Objective (SLO)", "A Service Level Indicator (SLI)", "An error budget"],
        "An SLI is the measurement, an SLO the internal target, and an SLA the contract with consequences."],
      ["arch", "A new contributor wants to help improve the storage features of Kubernetes. Where should they start?",
        ["With SIG Storage: its meetings and open issues", "With the CNCF Technical Oversight Committee", "With the CNCF Governing Board", "With a CNCF Technical Advisory Group"],
        "SIGs own the areas of the Kubernetes project and are open to everyone. The TOC, TAGs and Governing Board are CNCF bodies, not where Kubernetes code is planned."],
      ["arch", "A team wants a rule that all images must come from the internal registry. It wants to write the rule as a Kubernetes resource in YAML, without learning a policy language. Which project fits?",
        ["Kyverno", "Open Policy Agent with Gatekeeper", "Falco", "Trivy"],
        "Kyverno policies are Kubernetes resources. OPA policies are written in the Rego language, and Falco and Trivy do not control admission."],
      ["arch", "A platform team wants one place where developers find their services, documentation and templates for new projects. Which CNCF project provides such a developer portal?",
        ["Backstage", "Harbor", "Crossplane", "Dapr"],
        "Backstage is the developer portal project. Harbor is a registry, Crossplane manages infrastructure and Dapr provides application building blocks."]
    ]
  }
];
